<h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ)</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Choose the correct option from (a), (b), (c), or (d).</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Which of the following is not a self-adjusting force? <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Static friction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Limiting friction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Kinetic friction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Tension in a string</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> A body of mass  m  is placed on a rough surface with coefficient of static friction  &mu;<sub>s</sub> . The maximum force of static friction is given by: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  f<sub>s</sub> = &mu;<sub>s</sub> mg </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  f<sub>s</sub> < &mu;<sub>s</sub> mg </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  f<sub>s</sub> &le; &mu;<sub>s</sub> mg </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  f<sub>s</sub> > &mu;<sub>s</sub> mg </li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> An object of mass  10\ kg  is sliding on a frictionless surface with a constant velocity of  5\ m/s . The net force acting on the object is: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  50\ N </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  10\ N </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  0\ N </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  5\ N </li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> The apparent weight of a person inside a lift moving upward with an acceleration  a  is: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  m(g - a) </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  m(g + a) </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  mg </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  m &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">g<sup>2</sup> + a<sup>2</sup></span> </li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q5.</strong> A bullet of mass  0.04\ kg  moving with a speed of  90\ m/s  enters a heavy wooden block and is stopped within a distance of  60\ cm . The average resistive force exerted by the block on the bullet is: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  270\ N </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  540\ N </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  300\ N </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  810\ N </li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q6.</strong> Linear momentum of a particle is given by  <b>p&#8407;</b> = m<b>v&#8407;</b> . According to Newton's second law, the net force acting on the particle is equal to: <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">d<b>v&#8407;</b></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">dt</span></span> </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">d<b>p&#8407;</b></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">dt</span></span> </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  <b>p&#8407;</b> &times; <b>v&#8407;</b> </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  m <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">d<sup>2</sup><b>r&#8407;</b></span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">dt<sup>2</sup></span></span> </li></ul><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section B: Fill in the Blanks</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Fill in the blanks with appropriate terms.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q7.</strong> The property of a body by virtue of which it opposes any change in its state of rest or uniform motion is called _______. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q8.</strong> Banking of roads helps to provide the necessary _______ force for safe turning of vehicles without depending solely on friction. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section C: Assertion-Reason</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Read the Assertion (A) and Reason (R) statements and choose the correct option.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q9.</strong> Assertion (A): The acceleration of an object is zero if the net external force acting on it is zero.\nReason (R): Newton's first law of motion defines inertia and serves as a definition of inertial frames of reference. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[1M]</span></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are True and R is the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are True but R is not the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is True but R is False</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is False but R is True</li></ul><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section D: Very Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Answer in one or two sentences.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q10.</strong> State Newton's third law of motion and give one common example. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[2M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q11.</strong> Why are tyres of vehicles provided with irregular grooves or treads? <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[2M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q12.</strong> Define impulse of a force. State its relation with change in momentum. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[2M]</span></div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section E: Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Answer in 30 to 50 words.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q13.</strong> Explain why it is easier to pull a lawn mower than to push it. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[3M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q14.</strong> A batsman hits a ball straight back to the bowler without changing its initial speed of  12\ m/s . If the mass of the ball is  0.15\ kg , calculate the impulse imparted to the ball. (Assume linear motion) <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[3M]</span></div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q15.</strong> Derive the expression for the maximum safe speed of a vehicle on a banked circular road with friction coefficient  &mu; . <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[3M]</span></div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section F: Long Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Answer in detail with diagrams, steps, or derivations where applicable.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q16.</strong> State the Law of Conservation of Linear Momentum. Prove it using Newton's third law of motion for an isolated system of two interacting particles. Also, explain how recoil of a gun is an application of this law. <span style='float:right;font-weight:bold;color:#475569;'>[6M]</span></div>
